{"pair": "A-0", "difference": "possible", "evidence": "Both identify a core trade-off involving resource distribution, but A frames it as Equity vs. Efficiency or Sustainability vs. Immediate Needs based on resource type, while B emphasizes context-dependent trade-offs (utilitarian vs. survival, accuracy vs. time) without systematic categorization. A offers a more structured typology; B is more case-specific. Differences in framing and conceptual organization suggest possible divergence in strategy."}
{"pair": "A-1", "difference": "clear", "evidence": "A focuses on de-escalation, throttling, and survival via Minimum Viable Function, emphasizing stabilization through reduction. B centers on redundancy (shadow instance), automation (pre-scripted scripts), and graceful degradation. B's 3-layer model (Redundancy, Automation, Interface) introduces structural concepts absent in A. Clear difference in strategy: A prioritizes minimal survival; B emphasizes system resilience through duplication and automation."}
{"pair": "A-2", "difference": "clear", "evidence": "A proposes standardized solutions across contexts (version control, redundancy, documentation) and emphasizes cross-training and failover. B focuses on immediate communication, triage, and proactive rescheduling, prioritizing adaptability and real-time response. A assumes systemic fixes; B emphasizes interpersonal and dynamic adjustment. Clear difference in priorities: systemic robustness vs. flexible adaptation."}
{"pair": "B-0", "difference": "possible", "evidence": "A identifies a trade-off between immediate needs and long-term stability, advocating triage and mitigation. B introduces risk-mitigation planning with scenario analysis and frames the trade-off as Efficiency vs. Resilience or Immediate vs. Long-term Stability. Both converge on stability concerns, but B adds structured risk modeling. Differences in framing and conceptual tools suggest possible, not clear, divergence."}
{"pair": "B-1", "difference": "possible", "evidence": "A emphasizes triage, resource freeze, and external shielding, focusing on immediate stabilization and post-stress recovery. B advocates proactive preparation via clustering, buffering, and automated failover, with emphasis on pre-emptive infrastructure. Both address resilience during stress, but B's focus on distributed architecture and pre-stress clustering contrasts with A's reactive triage. Strategy differs in timing and structure—possible but not absolute divergence."}
{"pair": "B-2", "difference": "clear", "evidence": "A recommends a centralized scheduling system and cross-training, focusing on coordination and human redundancy. B proposes a technical 'fail-safe' mechanism with automatic backup switching, emphasizing system-level automation. A's solution is procedural and human-centered; B's is technical and system-centered. Clear difference in strategy and conceptual neighborhood."}
{"pair": "C-0", "difference": "possible", "evidence": "A frames the trade-off as Resource Efficiency vs. Risk Mitigation, balancing allocation with resilience. B explicitly prioritizes Risk Management and frames the trade-off as Safety/Stability vs. Efficiency/Speed, emphasizing survival. Both use risk language, but B more strongly prioritizes stability. Similar structure but differing emphasis—possible difference in priorities."}
{"pair": "C-1", "difference": "possible", "evidence": "A outlines a phased plan with task audit, handover, triage, and debrief, emphasizing documentation and decision authority. B structures around Mitigation, Automation, Communication, with pre-flight checklists and automated fail-safes. Both include handoff and monitoring, but B integrates 'graceful degradation' and headroom buffering. Overlap in goals, but B adds layered resilience—possible difference in framing and depth."}
{"pair": "C-2", "difference": "none", "evidence": "Both emphasize buffer time, modularity, cross-training, and monitoring. A uses 'flexible, modular, adaptive system' with buffer/slack, decoupling, and telemetry. B advocates buffer time, modular tasks, cross-training, and continuous check-ins. Nearly identical strategies and concepts. No meaningful difference in assumptions, priorities, or framing."}
